course: dictionary
lesson: 3586
title: Fibrous
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Fibrous comes from fibre, which means long, thin threads. Something fibrous is made of threads, or is full of them. Look at the words around it to check.
explain: «A coconut is a seed in a fibrous, air-filled husk that floats and keeps sea water out.» The words air-filled and floats give clues that the husk is light and made of threads. Swap in "made of threads" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Food can be fibrous too. Celery and old beans are tough and stringy, so they are fibrous. Fibre in food helps your body to digest it, which is why doctors and cooks talk about it.
explain: Plants have fibrous roots. «Grass has many thin fibrous roots near the surface.» Many thin roots spreading out are different from one thick root going straight down, like a carrot.
fact: Fibre means long, thin threads.
fact: Fibrous means made of fibres, or full of them.
fact: «Fibrous» can describe food that is tough and stringy, like celery.
fact: «Fibrous roots» are many thin roots spreading out, and «a taproot» is one thick root going down.
fact: Wood, cotton and muscle are all fibrous materials.
exercise: «A coconut is a seed in a fibrous, air-filled husk.» What does fibrous mean here? | *Made of long, thin threads | Heavy and solid like a stone | Smooth and shiny like glass | Sweet and juicy
exercise: «Grass has many thin fibrous roots near the surface.» How are these roots different from a carrot? | Both have one thick root going straight down | *Many thin roots spread out, but a carrot has one thick root | A carrot has many thin roots and grass has one thick root | Grass has no roots at all
exercise: Mum says celery is fibrous, so it is tough and stringy. Which food is also fibrous? | A soft bread roll | Jelly | *Old beans | Ice cream
test: «A dandelion has a thick deep taproot that stores food, so it grows back if you only pull off the leaves.» What is the difference between a taproot and fibrous roots? | A taproot grows in the air, but fibrous roots grow in the sea | A taproot is many thin roots, but fibrous roots are one thick root | There is no difference, they both mean the same | *A taproot is one thick root going deep, but fibrous roots are many thin roots spreading out
test: Ella says «fibrous» must mean soft and fluffy. Why is she wrong? | Fibrous means wet and sticky | Fibrous means soft and fluffy, so she is right | *Fibrous means made of threads, and tough foods like celery are fibrous too | Fibrous means very small
exam: «A coconut can float for months and still sprout.» What does fibrous most likely describe? | *The husk, which is made of threads and keeps water out | The seed inside, which is sweet and juicy | The sea, which is full of salt | The tree, which grows tall above the sand
